      "title": "Geometry of Triangles: The Pythagorean Theorem",
      "renderer": "manim",
      "narration": "Continuing our exploration of geometry, we now focus on another fundamental shape: the triangle. Specifically, we'll look at a special type of triangle called a right-angled triangle. This is a triangle where one of the three angles is exactly 90 degrees, like the corner of a square. The sides of a right-angled triangle have special names. The two sides that form the right angle are called the 'legs'. The third side, which is opposite the right angle and is always the longest side, is called the 'hypotenuse'. A truly ancient and incredibly useful discovery about right-angled triangles is the Pythagorean Theorem. This theorem reveals a fixed relationship between the lengths of the three sides. It states that the square of the length of the hypotenuse is equal to the sum of the squares of the lengths of the two legs. If we label the legs 'a' and 'b' and the hypotenuse 'c', the theorem can be written as the famous equation: a squared plus b squared equals c squared. This means if you know the lengths of any two sides of a right-angled triangle, you can always find the length of the third side. This theorem is a cornerstone of geometry and has countless applications in construction, navigation, and design.",
